      "message": "kernel: Add function for setting prune target in ChainstateManagerOptions\n\nCurrently, libbitcoinkernel's C API does not expose any mechanism to\nconfigure the block pruning target on btck_ChainstateManagerOptions,\neven though the underlying node::BlockManager::Options and\nnode::ChainstateLoadOptions fully support pruning. Consequently,\nattempting to use libbitcoinkernel with an existing pruned datadir\nfails during LoadChainstate() with an error directing the user to\nreindex.\n\nIntroduce btck_chainstate_manager_options_set_prune_target() to allow\nconfiguring the prune target on btck_ChainstateManagerOptions.\n\n- Disabling pruning is represented by 0 (the default).\n- Manual pruning is represented by btck_PRUNE_TARGET_MANUAL (matching\n  node::BlockManager::PRUNE_TARGET_MANUAL).\n- Automatic pruning requires at least btck_MIN_PRUNE_TARGET_BYTES (550 MiB,\n  matching MIN_DISK_SPACE_FOR_BLOCK_FILES).\n\nSetting a non-zero prune target sets opts.m_blockman_options.prune_target\nand enables opts.m_chainstate_load_options.prune under the options mutex,\nallowing libbitcoinkernel to open and operate on pruned datadirs.\n\nAlso adds C++ wrapper method SetPruneTarget() and unit tests validating\nboundary behavior in test_kernel.cpp.\n\nCloses #35627.",
      "committer": {
        "name": "Rahad Bhuiya",
        "email": "rahadbhuiya2021@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-10-23T17:27:44Z"
      },
