      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "Thanks for the thoughtful review and feedback @musaHaruna!\r\n\r\nGood catch on the test; resetting the target back to 0 right before constructing `ChainMan` only verified the option boundary validation rather than exercising `ChainstateManager` construction in pruned mode. I have updated the test to construct and verify `ChainMan` with a pruned configuration.\r\n\r\nRegarding the broader architectural discussion in #35627: the intent here was to provide the minimal options setter needed to unblock loading existing pruned datadirs (where `chainman.m_blockman.m_have_pruned` requires `options.prune` in `node::LoadChainstate`), while respecting `BlockManager`'s existing manual/target semantics.\r\n\r\nHowever, I completely understand the concern regarding full automatic pruning without application-level prune locks / retention controls in place. Would you recommend scoping this PR down strictly to an explicit compatibility opt-in (e.g. allowing existing pruned storage without exposing automatic disk-target pruning), or how would you prefer we structure this first step?",
      "user": {
        "login": "rahadbhuiya",
        "id": 62468406,
        "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjYyNDY4NDA2",
        "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/62468406?v=4",
